BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:vDouMh-rIPVjncCzslycZ
SUMMARY:Characterizing the role of Nephronectin during Anterior Eye Develop
	ment in Mice
DTSTAMP:20260805T053736Z
DTSTART:20260805T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Matthew Robert Garis  Doctoral Candidate  The
	sis Defense  Department: BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology  Locati
	on: Keck Hall 102  Anterior eye development is a complex process involving
	 the migration\, proliferation\, patterning and interactions between many 
	difference cell types in order to achieve the niche tissue properties requ
	ired for proper sight. The periocular mesenchyme interacts with nascent oc
	ular tissues and extracellular matrix proteins to provide a transparent co
	rnea. While support structures like sensory nerves and anterior eye vascul
	ature are formed and patterned with tight spatiotemporal regulation to sus
	tain ocular structures. Cellular interactions with the extracellular matri
	x are essential in regulating these morphogenic processes\, but which spec
	ific component proteins achieve these effects and the signaling pathways i
	nvolved are not well understood.  These studies were developed to further 
	understand the role of the extracellular matrix protein nephronectin (Npnt
	) during anterior eye development. While the protein has been researched d
	uring avian cornea development\, its role in mice was yet to be described.
	 The method by which these two organisms develop the cornea varies greatly
	 and this opened the potential to discover species-specific functions of N
	pnt. To this end\, I utilized a Npnt mutant mouse model to probe how the m
	urine cornea is formed without this matrix protein. I show that Npnt is ut
	ilized as a migration substrate by the periocular mesenchyme (POM)\, as it
	s absence leads to a reduction in corneal thickness and decreased cell cou
	nts but does not affect proliferation capacity. This result was confirmed 
	via in vitro POM explant experiments demonstrating the ability for these c
	ells to use Npnt as a substrate to migrate upon and blocking the EGFR path
	way abolished that capacity. Thus\, I found Npnt to mediate the migration 
	of POM cells into the murine cornea during development by activation of th
	e EGFR pathway.  Npnt has been shown to play a role in angiogenesis and se
	nsory nerve patterning during development but the protein's involvement in
	 these processes during anterior eye development has never been investigat
	ed. I show that Npnt has the correct spatiotemporal expression to potentia
	lly influence the migration and patterning of the vasculature and nerves t
	hat project to the anterior eye. Utilizing whole mount immunohistochemistr
	y experiments\, I found that Npnt is needed for the proper patterning of t
	he choroid vasculature and for the proper projection profile of sensory ne
	rve innervation into the anterior eye. I furthered these results using pri
	mary neuron cell culture experiments of dissociated trigeminal ganglia fro
	m embryonic mice\, discovering that Npnt attenuates migration of sensory n
	erves in an integrin dependent manner.  Finally\, I definitively determine
	d the developmental stage at which the corneal endothelial basement membra
	ne\, the Descemet's membrane (DM)\, is first formed with transmission elec
	tron microscopy. Npnt is expressed by the endothelial cells during develop
	ment and is integrated into the membrane with changing spatial expression 
	relative to other major basement membrane components. I found that Npnt is
	 expressed not only during development of the membrane but is sustained th
	roughout the life of an organism\, suggesting it may be involved in the ma
	intenance of the corneal endothelium or in the Descemet's membrane barrier
	 functions.  Overall\, in this thesis\, I identified a species-specific me
	chanism for how the extracellular matrix protein Npnt facilitates POM migr
	ation into the developing cornea. I expanded upon this by finding Npnt is 
	a substrate for endothelial vasculature and sensory nerves during anterior
	 eye formation. Identifying these functional roles for Npnt during develop
	ment and noting its continued expression in the Descemet's membrane provid
	es the basis for Npnt and its receptors being potential targets for cornea
	l cell-matrix therapies.  &#160\;  Matthew Garis is inviting you to a sche
	duled Zoom meeting.  Join Zoom Meeting  https://us05web.zoom.us/j/89012376
	861?pwd=cmQmWM057wr26gKLnnOxaqt4luW7aq.1  Meeting chat link  https://us05w
	eb.zoom.us/launch/jc/89012376861  Meeting ID: 890 1237 6861  Passcode: 5mM
	79E  (Department : BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology)\n\nhttps://e
	vents.rice.edu/event/435437-characterizing-the-role-of-nephronectin-during
	\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435437-characterizing-the-role-of-nephron
	ectin-during
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keck Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
