BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jZOH8m3-97MRn_qtCO2cf
SUMMARY:Parallel Algorithms for Performance Analysis at Exascale
DTSTAMP:20260805T053736Z
DTSTART:20260817T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Jonathon Anderson  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis
	 Defense  Department: Computer Science  Location: Duncan Hall 3012  Perfor
	mance analysis is critical for the development of high-performance computi
	ng (HPC) applications targeting exascale supercomputers. State-of-the-art 
	performance tools are unprepared to handle the complex and large applicati
	on binaries\, unprecedented performance data volume\, and widespread GPU a
	ccelerators on these platforms. Ultimately\, current tools fail to provide
	 detailed performance insights at the full scale of an exascale supercompu
	ter\, taking significant time or compute resources in postmortem processin
	g\, or sacrificing critical detail for responsiveness.  &#160\;  This diss
	ertation addresses these issues by accelerating key algorithms for postmor
	tem performance analysis\, these are correlation-bound problems where the 
	ample parallelism is limited by the need for "correlation" between inputs.
	 Our parallelization approach produces efficient\, dense parallelism for s
	uch problems by exploiting the reorderability between key operations in ca
	refully chosen data representations.  To handle large application binaries
	\, we introduce a novel parallel control flow graph (CFG) construction alg
	orithm with provable correctness and validated results. Our new implementa
	tion in Dyninst achieves parallel speedup as high as 25x\, and accelerates
	 a downstream performance analysis tool by up to 8x\, producing detailed b
	inary analysis for multi-gigabyte binaries in mere minutes.  To handle mas
	sive performance data at scale\, we introduce a new streaming aggregation 
	(SA) algorithm for performance data aggregation that provides efficient\, 
	dense shared-memory parallelism\, and a hybrid MPI/SA algorithm that incor
	porates conventional distributed-memory parallelism. The combination of SA
	 and sparse data formats developed by others accelerates HPCToolkit's anal
	ysis of large-scale application performance by as much as 81x. Using it\, 
	we successfully analyzed the performance of a real-world exascale applicat
	ion on a single compute node\, spending 0.012% of the application's comput
	e budget.  To handle complex heterogeneous code using GPU accelerators\, w
	e introduce a novel directed calling context graph (DCCG) representation f
	or CPU\, GPU\, and hybrid CPU/GPU calling context that provides detailed p
	erformance analysis for heterogeneous codes without costly space expansion
	. Combined with a hash-based identifier methodology to improve parallelism
	\, our new representation accelerates multi-node MPI/SA by as much as 2x. 
	 Much of the work described in this dissertation has been integrated into 
	upstream projects and deployed on real-world exascale platforms\, supporti
	ng the development of scientific HPC applications at exascale.  Zoom link:
	 https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/98347141781?pwd=tFAqtelOGMSQZkEx6wuWKXt3
	z4kKSQ.1  (Department : Computer Science)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event
	/436302-parallel-algorithms-for-performance-analysis-at\n\nFrom Rice Unive
	rsity - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/436302-parallel-algorithms-for-performanc
	e-analysis-at
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Duncan Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
